The conductivity of a solution can be measured can be measured by measuring the resistance offered by the solution within two electrodes in a conductivity cell. A Wheatstone bridge set up with alternating current is used as the principle behind a conductometer so that the value of a fourth resistance can be measured by a set of three known resistances so that no current flows in the galvanometer. The conductivity cell is made of a glass electrode made of coated platinum and is characterized by a cell constant $K_{cell}$ that depends on the length and area of the cell.
